{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347983364,"sessionID":"ses_f9e278886ffeEH0CF53I3JtGds","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_60a8f69b7c5b4bf49018a649","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-cannbench-kernel\">\n# Skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel\n\n# pyasc v2 CANNBench kernel workflow\n\nUse this skill only for the vendored benchmark under\n`integrations/cannbench/`. Its compact module contract overrides the generic\nstandalone-kernel workflow.\n\n## Non-negotiable contract\n\n- Treat `tasks/<op>/proto.yaml`, `golden.py`, and all 20 entries in\n  `cases.yaml` as one indivisible specification.\n- Produce one `candidate.py` with the exact top-level callable and defaults.\n- Use `torch` only for allocation, metadata views, and launches. Numerical work\n  belongs in one or more `@asc2.jit` kernels.\n- Import and call `ensure_npu_platform` from `._pyasc_runtime`.\n- Target `Ascend950PR_9599`, at most 72 vector cores, and UB capacity 253952 B.\n- For the pinned runtime use `asc2.global_tensor`, `asc2.copy_in`, and\n  `asc2.copy_out`; use `real_shape` for tails. Do not substitute the older\n  `tensor/load/store` spelling.\n- A production candidate normally uses bare `@asc2.jit` or an evidenced\n  allocation option. `always_compile=True` is a development aid, not a\n  submission requirement.\n\n## Required workflow\n\n1. Read the three task files and summarize every dtype, rank, attribute, list\n   arity, special value, and boundary shape. Do not optimize for one example.\n2. Read [operator patterns](references/operator-patterns.md), selecting only\n   the target operator section.\n3. Design host dispatch, kernel specializations, tails, dtype conversions, UB\n   budget, and numerical behavior before writing code.\n4. Implement the exact callable. Run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` and\n   the worker static contract check.\n5. Run the exact-v2 local compile gate for that operator as described in\n   [local validation](references/local-validation.md). A compile failure is\n   measured feedback; repair it and re-run all 20 cases.\n6. Have a different available model review the task, design, candidate, and\n   local report. Record both model IDs, OpenCode session IDs, loaded skill\n   source hashes, prompt/design/candidate hashes, and gate results.\n\nNever claim numerical correctness or performance from the local compile gate.\nOnly camodel execution can provide local numerical evidence; only CANNBench on\nthe real NPU is the acceptance/performance oracle.\n\n## Evidence labels\n\nUse exactly one label for every conclusion:\n\n- `verified-local-compile`: all task routes lower through pinned pyasc v2.\n- `verified-camodel`: outputs were executed and compared to the task golden.\n- `verified-cannbench`: official hardware result and case count are recorded.\n- `suspected`: evidence is incomplete or the root cause is inferred.\n\nDo not promote a locally qualified candidate into the canonical submission\npackage without numerical evidence. Do not spend a submission credit unless\nthe user explicitly enables remote evaluation.\n\n## Known limitations\n\nBefore using a workaround, consult\n[pyasc v2 blockers](references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md). Preserve full case\ncoverage when a case exposes a compiler limit; never remove or shrink the case\nto make a gate pass.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/local-validation.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/pyasc-v2-blockers.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel/references/operator-patterns.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-cannbench-kernel","dir":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/skills/pyasc-cannbench-kernel","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-cannbench-kernel","time":{"start":1788347983347,"end":1788347983360}},"id":"prt_061d8a549001eeSX18Fv8GyDbI","sessionID":"ses_f9e278886ffeEH0CF53I3JtGds","messageID":"msg_061d878a8001IQWIn5dojo07am"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347983496,"sessionID":"ses_f9e278886ffeEH0CF53I3JtGds","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"call_af9e46ab0a1047478cd299d0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"pyasc-syntax-constraints\">\n# Skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints\n\n# pyasc Syntax Constraints\n\nSyntax and keyword support varies across v2 snapshots. For CANNBench, the\nsource of truth is `integrations/cannbench/pyasc-v2-source` at the commit in\n`SOURCE_COMMIT`; load `pyasc-cannbench-kernel` and verify uncertain constructs\nagainst that source plus the exact-v2 compile gate. In particular, its\n`asc2.range` supports `unroll_factor` and `gm_barrier`, but not `parallel`.\n\n## Overview\n\npyasc compiles Python code to Ascend C via JIT. Only a subset of Python syntax is supported inside `@asc2.jit`-decorated functions. This skill documents what is and is not allowed.\n\n## Supported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Example | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| Attribute access | `nums.__len__()` | |\n| `while` loop | `while i < n: ...` | |\n| `for` loop | `for i in asc2.range(n): ...` | Use `asc2.range()` inside kernel, not `range()` |\n| Binary operators | `a + b * c` | `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `//`, `**`, `&`, `\\|`, `^`, `<<`, `>>` |\n| Unary operators | `~a`, `-a`, `not a` | |\n| `tuple` | `return x, y, z` | |\n| `list` | `nums = [a, b, c]` | |\n| `pass` | `pass` | |\n| Ternary `if` | `ans = x if x > 0 else 0` | |\n| `if`/`elif`/`else` | Full conditional blocks | |\n| Constants | `a = 1; b = True` | |\n| Comparisons | `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=` | |\n| Function calls | `func()` | Must be `@asc2.jit`-decorated or asc2 built-in |\n| Boolean logic | `and`, `or` | |\n| Augmented assign | `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `%=` | |\n| Assignment | `x = expr` | |\n| Annotated assign | `count: int = 3` | |\n| f-strings + assert | `assert cond, f\"msg {val}\"` | |\n| Subscript | `nums[0]` | |\n| Slice | `tensor[offset:]` | |\n\n## Unsupported Syntax\n\n| Syntax | Status | Workaround |\n|--------|--------|------------|\n| Nested functions | Not supported | Define separate `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `global` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| `return` from kernel | Not supported | Kernels write to output tensors; device functions may return |\n| `return` inside `if`/`for` | Not supported | Use top-level return only in device functions |\n| `continue` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `print()` | Not supported | Use `assert` with f-strings |\n| `with open(...)` | Not supported | Do file I/O outside JIT |\n| `raise` | Not supported | Use `assert` instead |\n| `try`/`except` | Not supported | Handle errors outside JIT |\n| `yield` / `yield from` | Not supported | No generators |\n| `lambda` | Not supported | Define named functions |\n| `break` | Not supported | Restructure loop logic |\n| `import` / `from...import` | Not supported | All imports outside JIT |\n| `async def` / `async with` | Not supported | No async |\n| `nonlocal` | Not supported | Pass values as parameters |\n| Class methods as kernels | Not supported | Use module-level functions |\n| If-scoped variables | Restricted | Initialize before `if`; use `asc.ConstExpr` for compile-time branching |\n| Unsigned int comparison | Restricted | Use `np.int64` or truthiness check instead |\n\n## Type Constraints\n\n### Kernel parameter types (runtime)\n\n| Supported | Not supported |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| `bool`, `int`, `float` | `str` |\n| `numpy` scalars and `ndarray` | `tuple`, `list`, `dict` |\n| `torch.Tensor` (NPU mode only) | Complex objects |\n| `asc.GlobalAddress` | |\n\n### Compile-time parameters\n\nUse `asc.ConstExpr[T]` for values that must be resolved at compile time:\n```python\n@asc2.jit(always_compile=True)\ndef kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    # tile_size is resolved at compile time and included in the JIT cache key\n    ...\n```\n\n### Built-in whitelist inside JIT\n\nOnly these built-ins are available: `dict`, `float`, `int`, `isinstance`, `issubclass`, `len`, `list`, `range`, `repr`, `str`, `tuple`, `type`\n\nNote: For iteration inside `@asc2.jit`, use `asc2.range()` instead of `range()`.\nDo not assume optional keywords are portable between pyasc snapshots.\n\n### Cube (matmul) loop + placement notes\n\n- **M/N/K tile loops are compile-time.** When the trip count is an\n  `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (matmul m/n/k tiles), a plain Python `for i in range(...)`\n  is fully unrolled at JIT time — that is the intended form for the loop that must\n  keep an operand resident in `L0A`/`L0B`. Use `asc2.range(...,\n  unroll_factor=2)` only on the *inner* tile loop you want software-pipelined\n  (double-buffered).\n- **Overlap (`gm_barrier=False`) doubles the pipelined L0 buffer.** The 2-deep\n  tile must fit half the L0 capacity (≤ 32 KiB for L0A/L0B, ≤ 64 KiB for the f32\n  L0C). A full-K `[256,128]` f16 L0B tile is 64 KiB and overflows when doubled\n  (`L0B overflow: 65536 bytes available, 131072 used`); shrink the tile (e.g.\n  `N_TILE=64`) before relying on overlap.\n- **Cube tile placement is explicit:** load/`asc2.copy` operands to\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L0A` (left) / `L0B` (right); stage from GM via\n  `asc2.TileLocation.L1` for reuse; the `@` result lands in L0C as f32 — cast with\n  `.to(asc2.float16)` before `asc2.store` if a 16-bit output is wanted.\n\n## Kernel vs Device Function Rules\n\n| Rule | Kernel function | Device function |\n|------|----------------|-----------------|\n| Launched from | Host via `kernel[core_num](...)` | Other `@asc2.jit` functions |\n| `return` value | Not allowed | Allowed |\n| `@asc2.jit(...)` options | Effective (always_compile, etc.) | Ignored |\n| Nesting | Cannot be nested | Cannot be nested |\n\n## References\n\n- [Supported Syntax Details](references/supported-syntax.md)\n- [Unsupported Syntax Details](references/unsupported-syntax.md)\n- [Type Constraints](references/type-constraints.md)\n- [ConstExpr Guide](references/constexpr-guide.md)\n\nBase directory for this skill: /home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/supported-syntax.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/type-constraints.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/constexpr-guide.md</file>\n<file>/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints/references/unsupported-syntax.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"pyasc-syntax-constraints","dir":"/home/aloschilov/.config/opencode/skills/pyasc-syntax-constraints","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: pyasc-syntax-constraints","time":{"start":1788347983489,"end":1788347983495}},"id":"prt_061d8a603001qk9nYQsDbwYTU4","sessionID":"ses_f9e278886ffeEH0CF53I3JtGds","messageID":"msg_061d878a8001IQWIn5dojo07am"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788347983898,"sessionID":"ses_f9e278886ffeEH0CF53I3JtGds","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f2633cf17d194f55b3e76831","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/sigmoid-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/sigmoid-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **Sigmoid**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `sigmoid` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # Sigmoid 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: Sigmoid 算子对输入张量完成 Sigmoid 运算，将任意实数映射到 (0, 1) 区间。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - 二分类任务的输出层激活\n19: - 门控机制（LSTM、GRU 等 RNN 中的门控信号）\n20: - 注意力权重计算\n21: - 概率输出的归一化\n22: \n23: **算子特征**：\n24: - 难度等级：L1（Elementwise）\n25: - 单输入单输出，逐元素运算，输出 shape 与输入完全一致\n26: - 支持 ND 格式输入\n27: \n28: ## 2. 算子定义\n29: \n30: ### 数学公式\n31: \n32: $$\n33: y = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-x}}\n34: $$\n35: \n36: ### 特殊情况\n37: \n38: | 输入 | 输出 |\n39: |------|------|\n40: | x = 0 | y = 0.5 |\n41: | x → +∞ | y → 1 |\n42: | x → -∞ | y → 0 |\n43: \n44: ## 3. 接口规范\n45: \n46: ### 算子原型\n47: \n48: ```python\n49: cann_bench.sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n50: ```\n51: \n52: ### 输入参数说明\n53: \n54: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n55: |------|------|--------|------|\n56: | x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量，支持 ND 格式 |\n57: \n58: ### 输出\n59: \n60: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n61: |------|-------|-------|------|\n62: | y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | Sigmoid 激活结果 |\n63: \n64: ### 数据类型\n65: \n66: | 输入 dtype | 输出 dtype |\n67: |-----------|-----------|\n68: | float16 | float16 |\n69: | float32 | float32 |\n70: | bfloat16 | bfloat16 |\n71: \n72: ### 规则与约束\n73: \n74: - 输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，输出 dtype 与输入 dtype 一致\n75: - 无额外属性参数\n76: \n77: ### 支持范围\n78: \n79: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n80: \n81: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n82: |---|---|---|\n83: | `x` 维度数 (ndim) | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1 ~ 5 |\n84: | `x` 单维大小 | 1 ~ 16384 | cases.csv 实测 2 ~ 8193 |\n85: | `x` 总元素数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ 64M  |\n86: \n87: 约束：输出 shape 与输入 shape 完全一致，无 broadcasting；无额外属性。\n88: \n89: ## 4. 精度要求\n90: \n91: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n92: \n93: **误差指标**：\n94: \n95: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n96: \n97:    $$\n98:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n99:    $$\n100: \n101: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n102: \n103:    $$\n104:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n105:    $$\n106: \n107: **通过标准**：\n108: \n109: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n110: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n111: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n112: \n113: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n114: \n115: \n116: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n117: \n118: ```python\n119: import torch\n120: \n121: def sigmoid(\n122:     x: torch.Tensor\n123: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n124:     \"\"\"\n125:     对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n126: \n127:     公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n128: \n129:     Args:\n130:         x: 输入张量\n131: \n132:     Returns:\n133:         输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n134:     \"\"\"\n135: \n136:     y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n137:     return y\n138: ```\n139: \n140: ## 6. 额外信息\n141: \n142: ### 算子调用示例\n143: \n144: ```python\n145: import torch\n146: import cann_bench\n147: \n148: x = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float32, device=\"npu\")\n149: y = cann_bench.sigmoid(x)\n150: ```\n151: \n152: ## proto.yaml\n153: \n154: ```yaml\n155: operator:\n156:   name: Sigmoid\n157:   category: Elementwise\n158:   difficulty: L1\n159:   formula: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n160:   description: 对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n161:   shape_support: 输入ND格式\n162:   attrs: []\n163:   inputs:\n164:   - name: x\n165:     description: 输入张量\n166:     dtype:\n167:     - float32\n168:     - float16\n169:     - bfloat16\n170:   outputs:\n171:   - name: y\n172:     description: 输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n173:     dtype:\n174:     - float32\n175:     - float16\n176:     - bfloat16\n177:   schema: sigmoid(Tensor x) -> Tensor y\n178: ```\n179: \n180: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n181: \n182: ```python\n183: #!/usr/bin/python3\n184: # coding=utf-8\n185: \n186: \n187: import torch\n188: \n189: \"\"\"\n190: Sigmoid算子Torch Golden参考实现\n191: \n192: 对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n193: 公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n194: \"\"\"\n195: def sigmoid(\n196:     x: torch.Tensor\n197: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n198:     \"\"\"\n199:     对输入Tensor完成Sigmoid运算\n200:     \n201:     公式: y = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))\n202:     \n203:     Args:\n204:         x: 输入张量\n205:     \n206:     Returns:\n207:         输出张量，Sigmoid激活结果\n208:     \"\"\"\n209: \n210:     y = torch.sigmoid(x)\n211:     return y\n212: ```\n213: \n214: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n215: \n216: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n217: |---|---|---|---|---|\n218: | 1 | [[1024, 1024]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 1] | - |\n219: | 2 | [[2048, 2048]] | ['float32'] | [-2, 2] | - |\n220: | 3 | [[4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 3] | - |\n221: | 4 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float16'] | [-10, 10] | - |\n222: | 5 | [[8192, 8192]] | ['float32'] | [-100, 100] | - |\n223: | 6 | [[1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-0.1, 0.1] | - |\n224: | 7 | [[1009, 1021]] | ['float16'] | [-1, 2] | - |\n225: | 8 | [[1537, 769]] | ['float32'] | [-5, 10] | - |\n226: | 9 | [[363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-50, 100] | - |\n227: | 10 | [[2049, 513]] | ['float16'] | [-65504, 65504] | - |\n228: | 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4001]] | ['float32'] | [-88, 88] | - |\n229: | 12 | [[1000003]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-inf, inf] | - |\n230: | 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32'] | [nan, nan] | - |\n231: | 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1009]] | ['float16'] | [0, 0] | - |\n232: | 15 | [[512, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.5, 0.5] | - |\n233: | 16 | [[255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-1, 3] | - |\n234: | 17 | [[4097, 511]] | ['float16'] | [-1000, 1000] | - |\n235: | 18 | [[2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32'] | [-0.2, 0.2] | - |\n236: | 19 | [[4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16'] | [-3, 6] | - |\n237: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float32'] | [-20, 40] | - |\n238: \n239: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n240: \n241: ```python\n242: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n243: \n244: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n245:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n246:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n247:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n248:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n249:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n250:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n251:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n252:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n253:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n254: \"\"\"\n255: \n256: import torch\n257: \n258: import asc\n259: import asc2\n260: \n261: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n262: \n263: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n264: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n265: _MAX_CORES = 72\n266: \n267: \n268: @asc2.jit\n269: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n270:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n271:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n272:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n273:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n274:                         unroll_factor=2):\n275:         off = t * tile_size\n276:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n277:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n278:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n279:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n280:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n281: \n282: \n283: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n284:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n285:     ensure_npu_platform()\n286:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n287:         x = x.contiguous()\n288:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n289:     size = x.numel()\n290:     if size == 0:\n291:         return out\n292:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n293:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n294:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n295:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n296:     else:\n297:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n298:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n299:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n300:     return out\n301: \n302: ```\n303: \n304: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n305: \n306: ## Module shape\n307: \n308: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n309: \n310: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n311:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n312: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n313: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n314: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n315:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n316:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n317:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n318:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n319: \n320: ## Kernel authoring rules\n321: \n322: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n323:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n324:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n325: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n326:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n327:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n328: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n329: \n330: ```python\n331: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n332:     off = t * tile_size\n333:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n334:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n335:     ...compute on tiles...\n336:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n337: ```\n338: \n339: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n340:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n341: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n342:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n343:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n344:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n345:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n346:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n347:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n348:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n349:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n350:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n351:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n352: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n353:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n354:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n355: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n356:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n357: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n358:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n359: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n360:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n361:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n362:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n363:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n364:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n365:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n366:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n367:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n368:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n369:   cases).\n370: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n371:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n372: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n373:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n374:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n375:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n376:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n377:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n378:   TypeError).\n379: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n380:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n381:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n382:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n383:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n384:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n385:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n386: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n387:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n388:   style (min 32 bytes).\n389: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n390:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n391:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n392: \n393: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n394: \n395: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n396:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n397:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0